menu "airkvm"

choice AIRKVM_BOARD
    prompt "Target board"
    # Gated to the targets that actually have a board. Without this the choice
    # still *parses* on other targets — an esp32s2 configure completes cleanly,
    # verified — but it offers nothing selectable, so no CONFIG_AIRKVM_BOARD_*
    # is set and the failure surfaces later, at board.h's #error. Hiding an
    # empty choice is honest: a target with no board should not appear to have
    # a configurable one.
    #
    # Deliberately NOT extended to esp32s2 even though the HID backend below
    # offers it: no S2 board exists. Adding an option for hardware nobody has
    # would be the kind of implied support AGENTS.md §4 forbids.
    depends on IDF_TARGET_ESP32S3 || IDF_TARGET_ESP32C3
    default AIRKVM_BOARD_S3_OLED042 if IDF_TARGET_ESP32S3
    default AIRKVM_BOARD_C3_EGG if IDF_TARGET_ESP32C3
    help
        Which physical board this image is for.

        This is a separate axis from IDF_TARGET on purpose: two different
        boards can share a chip. main/board.h used to key its pin map off
        CONFIG_IDF_TARGET_*, which worked only while there was at most one
        board per chip — a second ESP32-S3 board collides on that arm and
        silently inherits the first one's pins.

config AIRKVM_BOARD_S3_OLED042
    bool "ESP32-S3-0.42 OLED supermini (the dongle)"
    depends on IDF_TARGET_ESP32S3
    help
        The shipping dongle. SSD1306 72x40 status panel on I2C, no LED.

config AIRKVM_BOARD_S3_LCD147
    bool "Waveshare ESP32-S3-LCD-1.47B"
    depends on IDF_TARGET_ESP32S3
    help
        ST7789 172x320 IPS LCD on SPI, 16MB flash, 8MB octal PSRAM, WS2812
        RGB LED, microSD, QMI8658 IMU. Only the LCD is driven today.

        Needs sdkconfig.lcd147 layered on top of sdkconfig.defaults for its
        flash size — see mise.toml's build-lcd147 task. Selecting this board
        alone is not enough.

config AIRKVM_BOARD_C3_EGG
    bool "ESP32-C3 \"egg\" bench board"
    depends on IDF_TARGET_ESP32C3
    help
        Bench board only. Cannot be a USB keyboard (no OTG device
        controller), so it pairs with the log HID backend and exercises
        everything except being a keyboard.

endchoice

choice AIRKVM_HID_BACKEND
    prompt "HID output backend"
    default AIRKVM_HID_BACKEND_USB if IDF_TARGET_ESP32S3 || IDF_TARGET_ESP32S2
    default AIRKVM_HID_BACKEND_LOG
    help
        How decoded input reaches the target machine.

config AIRKVM_HID_BACKEND_USB
    bool "USB HID (TinyUSB)"
    depends on IDF_TARGET_ESP32S3 || IDF_TARGET_ESP32S2
    help
        Real USB HID keyboard + mouse. Requires native USB OTG, which only the
        S3 and S2 have — the option is not offered on other targets because no
        firmware can add the hardware.

config AIRKVM_HID_BACKEND_LOG
    bool "Bench: log intents, drive nothing"
    help
        No HID output at all. Decoded input is logged instead, so the WiFi,
        console, WebSocket, and protocol paths can be tested on any board.
        This does NOT turn a non-OTG board into a keyboard; it is how you test
        everything except being a keyboard.

endchoice

choice AIRKVM_THEME
    prompt "Status panel colour theme"
    # Offered only on the board that has a palette. The 0.42" OLED is 1bpp:
    # every ink role renders as one lit pixel there, so a theme is a genuine
    # no-op and showing a choice would be exactly the kind of implied support
    # AGENTS.md §4 forbids — same reasoning as the board choice above hiding
    # itself on targets with no board.
    depends on AIRKVM_BOARD_S3_LCD147
    default AIRKVM_THEME_MONOKAI_MACHINE
    help
        Which four colours main/ui.c's ink roles are drawn in.

        Build-time rather than runtime, and that is a proposal rather than a
        settled convention — main/theme.h states the argument, the short form
        being that the BOOT button's two meanings are already load-bearing for
        risk R-006 and a console control costs a protocol frame plus NVS.

        Themes are ordered by their WEAKEST contrast ratio, best first. The
        ratios are in main/theme.h and are asserted, not asserted-in-prose, by
        test/host/test_theme.c.

config AIRKVM_THEME_TOKYO_NIGHT
    bool "Tokyo Night"
    help
        Dark blue-violet. 11.2:1 / 8.5:1 / 10.3:1 (primary / secondary /
        accent). The highest floor of the five — but only 2.7 between primary
        and secondary, so the address does not visibly outrank the context
        around it. Pick this if you want everything maximally legible and do
        not care which line you read first.

config AIRKVM_THEME_MONOKAI_MACHINE
    bool "Monokai Pro (Filter Machine)"
    help
        Dark desaturated teal. 13.5:1 / 7.9:1 / 8.4:1, and the default. Its
        floor is slightly below Tokyo Night's, but it has more than twice the
        separation between primary and secondary, so the IP address reads as
        the thing to read. See main/theme.h for why the rendered preview was
        allowed to overrule the ranking.

config AIRKVM_THEME_ONE_DARK_NIGHT_FLAT
    bool "One Dark Pro Night Flat"
    help
        Near-black neutral. 8.5:1 / 4.8:1 / 7.6:1. The lowest secondary of the
        dark themes — over the 4.5:1 floor, but with the least margin.

config AIRKVM_THEME_MONOKAI_LIGHT
    bool "Monokai Pro (light)"
    help
        The only light theme. 14.4:1 / 5.0:1 / 3.6:1, and the most readable of
        the five in daylight.

        Note what light means on this panel: every undrawn pixel is ink role 0,
        so this puts a lit 172x320 rectangle on the desk. Excellent on a bench,
        poor in a dark rack.

config AIRKVM_THEME_SOLARIZED_DARK
    bool "Solarized Dark"
    help
        What the first coloured build shipped, kept for anyone who liked it.
        5.9:1 / 5.0:1 / 5.0:1 — the flattest theme here, with only 0.9 between
        primary and secondary, which is a property of Solarized rather than
        something this project can tune out.

        Its secondary role is base0 (body text) and not base01 (comments),
        which is what the original used and what measured 2.9:1.

endchoice

config AIRKVM_MSC_SD
    bool "Expose the microSD slot to the target as the mass-storage medium"
    depends on AIRKVM_BOARD_S3_LCD147 && AIRKVM_HID_BACKEND_USB
    default y
    help
        Boards with a card slot only. An inserted card becomes the medium of
        the USB mass-storage LUN (read-only in this version); pulling it is a
        removal. A browser-attached image and the card are exclusive — the
        first to arrive holds the LUN.

        This is a trust-boundary change on top of R-008 (docs/repo-map.md):
        a card is input the firmware did not author. Read-only means it
        cannot carry state off the device yet.

config AIRKVM_TLS
    bool "Serve the console over HTTPS/WSS"
    default y
    help
        Serves the console over TLS using the certificate in certs/, generated
        by tools/make_certs.sh.

        Two reasons, and the second is the one that bites if you turn this off:
        keystrokes otherwise cross the network in plaintext for anyone on the
        LAN to read (risk R-007), and browsers refuse camera access to
        non-secure origins, so the console's capture-device dropdown is empty
        over plain HTTP.

        Costs roughly 40KB of heap per connection and about a second per
        handshake on a C3. Turning it off is reasonable for bench work on a
        board with no certificate provisioned.

config AIRKVM_WIFI_SSID
    string "WiFi SSID"
    default ""
    help
        Network the dongle joins. Required — there is deliberately no default,
        because a plausible-looking wrong value fails as a radio problem rather
        than as a config problem (AGENTS.md §8).

config AIRKVM_WIFI_PASSWORD
    string "WiFi password"
    default ""
    help
        WPA2 passphrase. Empty means an open network.

config AIRKVM_MDNS_HOSTNAME
    string "mDNS hostname"
    default "airkvm"
    help
        The console is reachable at http://<hostname>.local/. Give each dongle a
        distinct name if you run more than one, or they will fight over it.

config AIRKVM_OTA_TEST_URL
    string "OTA test image URL (throwaway, dev only)"
    default ""
    help
        If set, polled every 60s once WiFi associates: each poll reads only
        the served image's header and compares it against the running image,
        downloading and rebooting into it only on a real difference. Proves
        the OTA primitive (fetch, write the inactive slot, switch boot
        partition, roll back on failure) works on real hardware.

        This is NOT the fleet delivery mechanism (docs/design/fleet.md item
        4): no signing, no coordinator, no mTLS tunnel, and a URL baked into
        the image at build time rather than supplied by anything at runtime —
        network or otherwise. That is deliberate: this device's console is
        unauthenticated by design (R-001), and a network-reachable "fetch
        this URL and flash it" trigger would be a materially different risk
        than anything else this firmware currently exposes. Leave empty for
        every build except a deliberate local OTA test.

        A plain http:// URL here also needs CONFIG_ESP_HTTPS_OTA_ALLOW_HTTP
        enabled separately (Component config > ESP HTTPS OTA), which this
        project's own config never sets — set it in a local sdkconfig only,
        never commit it. Without it every poll fails before touching the
        network (ESP_ERR_INVALID_ARG), which looks like a network problem.

config AIRKVM_LOG_TAP
    bool "Serve the firmware log over plain HTTP (diagnostic builds only)"
    depends on AIRKVM_TLS
    default n
    help
        Requires AIRKVM_TLS, because it serves on port 80 and a non-TLS
        console is already there (start_http in main/net.c). Without this
        dependency the two race for the same port and which one you get is
        a coin toss — the log tap is a diagnostic, so it must never be the
        thing that breaks the console it is diagnosing.

        Captures ESP_LOG output into a ring buffer and serves it at
        http://<ip>/log on port 80, alongside whatever the console is doing.

        Exists because this board can reach a state with no readable log at
        all: a USB HID build has TinyUSB on the only port, and after a soft
        reboot the USB PHY does not return to USB-Serial/JTAG without a
        physical power cycle. When the thing being debugged is why the console
        will not start (issue #24), the network is the only channel left.

        NEVER enable this in anything resembling a real build. It serves
        unfiltered firmware logs to any unauthenticated LAN client, which is
        strictly worse than R-001: R-001 lets someone type at the target, this
        lets them read what the device is doing. The keystroke debug row
        (R-006) is gated on physical presence for exactly this reason.

endmenu
